From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tim Ansell <me@mith.ro>
Date: Sat, 11 Jul 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ftgmac100: reset RMII PHY on AST2050 to enable RX datapath

The Aspeed AST2050 (G3) MAC is register-compatible with the AST2400 but
its RMII RX datapath is not active after the bootloader handoff: the MAC
RX engine pulls zero frames off the RMII interface (TX works, link is up
at 100Mbps, the RX ring is valid and RXDMA_EN|RXMAC_EN are set, yet rx=0)
until the OS resets and reconfigures the RMII PHY. The generic aspeed path
does no RMII setup for this SoC -- it only warns "Unsupported PHY mode
rmii" and assumes firmware configured it -- so mainline never brings eth0
RX up on the real AST2050.

Recognise the "aspeed,ast2050-mac" compatible and, for it, reset the RMII
PHY in ftgmac100_mii_probe() to (re)establish the RMII RX clock/datapath.

Signed-off-by: Tim Ansell <me@mith.ro>
---
diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/faraday/ftgmac100.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/faraday/ftgmac100.c
index 848e41a4b..bb8cb73cb 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/faraday/ftgmac100.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/faraday/ftgmac100.c
@@ -109,6 +109,7 @@ struct ftgmac100 {
 	/* Misc */
 	bool need_mac_restart;
 	bool is_aspeed;
+	bool is_ast2050;
 };
 
 static int ftgmac100_reset_mac(struct ftgmac100 *priv, u32 maccr)
@@ -1484,6 +1485,25 @@ static int ftgmac100_mii_probe(struct net_device *netdev)
 	 */
 	phy_support_asym_pause(phydev);
 
+	/*
+	 * AST2050 (G3) RMII RX bring-up. On the AST2050 the RMII RX datapath is
+	 * not active after the bootloader hands off: the MAC RX engine pulls
+	 * zero frames off the RMII interface (eth0 TX works, link reports up at
+	 * 100Mbps, the RX ring is valid and RXDMA_EN|RXMAC_EN are set, yet
+	 * rx=0) until the OS resets and reconfigures the RMII PHY. The generic
+	 * aspeed path deliberately does no RMII setup for this SoC -- it only
+	 * warns "Unsupported PHY mode rmii" above and assumes firmware left the
+	 * interface configured -- which is why the mainline driver never brings
+	 * RX up on the real AST2050. Reset the PHY here to (re)establish the
+	 * RMII RX clock/datapath. phylib reconfigures speed/duplex on the
+	 * subsequent phy_start(), so this only re-arms the RMII RX side.
+	 */
+	if (priv->is_ast2050 && phy_intf == PHY_INTERFACE_MODE_RMII) {
+		netdev_info(netdev,
+			    "AST2050: resetting RMII PHY to enable RX datapath\n");
+		phy_write(phydev, MII_BMCR, BMCR_RESET);
+	}
+
 	/* Display what we found */
 	phy_attached_info(phydev);
 
@@ -1864,7 +1884,18 @@ static int ftgmac100_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 		goto err_phy_connect;
 
 	np = pdev->dev.of_node;
-	if (np && (of_device_is_compatible(np, "aspeed,ast2400-mac") ||
+	if (np && of_device_is_compatible(np, "aspeed,ast2050-mac")) {
+		/*
+		 * AST2050 (G3): register-compatible with the AST2400 MAC (same
+		 * BIT(30) EDORR/EDOTR ring-end bits) but its RMII RX datapath
+		 * needs an explicit PHY reset in ftgmac100_mii_probe() -- see
+		 * there. Flagged separately so only the G3 gets that quirk.
+		 */
+		priv->rxdes0_edorr_mask = BIT(30);
+		priv->txdes0_edotr_mask = BIT(30);
+		priv->is_aspeed = true;
+		priv->is_ast2050 = true;
+	} else if (np && (of_device_is_compatible(np, "aspeed,ast2400-mac") ||
 		   of_device_is_compatible(np, "aspeed,ast2500-mac") ||
 		   of_device_is_compatible(np, "aspeed,ast2600-mac"))) {
 		priv->rxdes0_edorr_mask = BIT(30);
